Repository evidence:
[Section: 16. Source tree / software architecture > Architectural weaknesses]

- no clear module boundaries;

- state transitions are not represented as an explicit FSM/table;

- menu/UI and control rules are tightly coupled;

- no sensor abstraction;

- no typed enums for state values;

- correctness depends on raw characters and condition ordering.

[Section: 23. Mistakes / anti-patterns / likely lessons — expanded]

The defects form a coherent set of early-C lessons.

[Section: 23. Mistakes / anti-patterns / likely lessons — expanded > Portability risk]

Using plain `char` for values that may exceed signed-char range can produce implementation-dependent behavior.

[Section: 31. Product / system failure potential]

In this repository the “vehicle” is simulated, so direct physical harm is absent.

If equivalent logic were transplanted into a real control system, the defects would be unacceptable:

- wrong branch behavior;

- incorrect input representation;

- potential signed-range issues;

- no validation;

- no tests.

This contrast is analytically useful: **domain vocabulary must never cause educational code to be mistaken for safety-critical engineering evidence.**

